ethiopia's government has declared a  national state of emergency and ordered  residents of addis ababa to prepare to  defend their neighborhoods as the grain  forces make rapid gains and advance  towards the capital  fighters from the northern tigrai region  say they have seized two cities about  400 kilometers away from addis ababa the  rebels have been locked in a brutal war  with government forces since last year  and the conflict has plunged hundreds of  thousands of people integra into famine  left thousands of civilians dead and  forced millions to flee their homes  just days after the latest deadly  airstrike in the tigrian capital the  ethiopian government is warning that  fighting may soon reach ethiopia's  capital city addis ababa some residents  fear the advance to grind fighters who  are now reported less than 250 miles  from the capital others support the  government's decision  the state of emergency is to protect the  nation's existence unity and sovereignty  with security  [Music]  i do not think it will harm or benefit  any individuals  as  well after a year of brutal fighting  thousands of civilians are dead while  millions have had to flee their homes  the un has warned that all sites in the  conflict have committed severe abuses  all parties to the tigray conflict have  committed violations of international  human rights humanitarian and refugee  law  some of these may amount to war crimes  and crimes against humanity but with  soldiers on the move and both sides  showing no willingness to compromise the  ethiopians caught in the middle will  continue to suffer  elizabeth rossell is a spokesperson for  the united nations human rights office  and she joins us now from geneva  switzerland  i'm going to start right off the bat  with you how worried are you about the  situation in ethiopia because we're  getting reports that rebels of the  tagraya people's liberation front  say they are close to capturing addis  ababa  well it is clearly a very worrying  situation and it's deteriorating  and this underscores the the importance  of what we've been saying today that  during the conflict which began last  november  the all parties to the conflict have  committed violations violations such as  attacks on civilians extrajudicial  killings torture and of course rape lots  of sexual violence and gender-based  violence against women we are concerned  the report that we've issued today went  up to the end of june  but we have put out also information  today talking about what has happened  since the conflict dynamics changed at  the end of june but we continue to see  violations and abuses being committed by  the ethiopian air australian forces and  by tigran forces we are very worried and  that's why the un high commissioner for  human rights michelle bashary today made  an impassioned plea for the violence to  stop and for the parties to ensure the  protection of civilians now ethiopian  prime minister abi ahmed we understand  has urged residents to arm themselves  and defend  this how dangerous is it if uh the line  between civilians and combatants is  blurred  well from a human rights perspective it  is incredibly important we always speak  about the impact of conflict on  civilians and it is clear that when  conflict is waged that the parties must  take all precautions to protect  civilians and that is something that we  have not seen we did not see and we have  documented it in our report there were  indiscriminate attacks on civilian  places such as schools and hospitals and  places of worship we heard from victims  and witnesses how people were killed in  shelling we've heard a whole range of  abuses and violations documented and  told to us by the people that we spoke  to for our report  now as you know there there has been no  let up in fighting there is an  intensification as a matter of fact  taking place who could mediate a  potential ceasefire the african union  the u.n  well i think it is very important that  of course the the parties to the  conflict in integrity uh get together  that they they  have a ceasefire that they are able to  engage in inclusive dialogue it is also  clearly important that the international  community plays a role because things  are deteriorating it is incredibly  worrying and our report today does lay  bare the complete  devastating impact that the conflict has  had and continues to have on civilians  remember the humanitarian situation in  the country is absolutely dire across  tigray afar and amhara there are seven  million people who are in dire need of  humanitarian assistance five million  alone in tigre and four hundred thousand  of those are in famic-like conditions so  it's absolutely urgent that the fighting  stops that there is a ceasefire and that  the parties get around the table to to  to talk and also that the parties  are able to investigate and hold  accountable those who have perpetrated  human rights violations and abuses now  it seems that all parties are treating  this strife as existential some  observers are calling the atrocities  that are happening ethiopia a genocide  but that word has not been used in this  uh report  is the u.n shying away from using such  terminology  no not shying away at all what the  report lays out is that what we found  the the the torture the indiscriminate  attacks the extrajudicial killings and  other violations and abuses may amount  to war crimes and crimes against  humanity and these are very serious  atrocity crimes now we don't speak of  genocide in a report but we do flag that  they they appear to be in instances of  ethnically driven violence and we're  saying that there needs to be further  investigation of this but just to  underscore we're saying that war crimes  and crimes against humanity may have  been committed and these are incredibly  serious crimes indeed  liz thrussell is a spokesperson for the  united nations human rights office in  geneva switzerland and she's been  talking to us about the un report on the  conflict in ethiopia thank you very much  for joining us thank you for your time  thank you
 

